# Rust implementation style (tar-dedup)

## One function when asked for one function

When the user asks for **a** function (or "single function", "one populate function", etc.), implement **one** function. Do not split into helpers, wrappers, or parallel code paths unless the user asks or the language truly forces it (e.g. test-only modules).

```rust
// ❌ User asked for one populate function; agent added populate_abs, populate_rel,
//    catalog_to_target_abs, ancestor_target_dirs, and thin wrappers.

// ✅ One public entry point; keep logic inline or in existing shared helpers only
//    when they already exist in the codebase.
pub fn populate_out_tree(db: &Database, config: &ExtractConfig, shutdown: &Shutdown) -> Result<()> {
    // ...
}
```

Prefer extending an existing function over introducing a small helper file or module.

## Errors vs contracts — use `expect`, not defensive `?` spam

Distinguish **user/input fault** from **internal contract violation**:

| Situation | Use |
|-----------|-----|
| User config, CLI, paths, I/O the user controls | `Result` / `Error` propagated to caller |
| Invariant the program establishes (schema init, prior pipeline stage, non-empty batch after check, FK row inserted by `ingest_filters` before `apply_no_filter`) | `expect("…")` or `debug_assert!` |

Do **not** wrap every step in `map_err`, `ok_or_else`, or nested `Error::Config` when failure means a bug or broken invariant, not bad user input.

```rust
// ❌ Contract: batch is non-empty after is_empty check
let last = batch.last().ok_or_else(|| Error::Config("empty batch".into()))?;

// ✅
let last = batch.last().expect("non-empty batch");
```

```rust
// ❌ Do not "fix" pipeline functions by inserting rows the real pipeline already creates
//    (e.g. extra filter_reason INSERT inside apply_no_filter).

// ✅ Call the same setup the production path uses (e.g. ingest_filters), or rely on
//    preconditions documented at the call site.
```

Before changing core pipeline code, read the **call site and upstream setup** (often a few lines above). Do not patch symptoms in leaf functions when the caller already guarantees the precondition.

## Tests

Test helpers may call production setup (`ingest_filters`, etc.) instead of reimplementing or weakening invariants in library code.
